# verb-kind-lint.ratchet -- commands that were already in the verb build when
# the declaration rule landed, and are forgiven until their own project adds
# `# KIND: verb` to its bin/<name>. See bin/verb-kind-lint.sh.
#
# SHRINKS ONLY. `--accept` drops entries whose command has declared itself or
# left the build; it will NOT add one. Growing this file is a hand edit, on
# purpose, so that it is reviewed. A command that is not listed here and
# declares nothing fails on its first night -- which is the whole requirement,
# since the next product to ship down the workchain will be a NEW arrival and
# not one of these.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RE-SEEDED 2026-08-11 FROM THE BUILD THAT IS CURRENT ON THE DAY IT LANDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The first seed was taken on 2026-08-08 against build 2026-08-06T003928Z: 32
# commands, 12 projects. This branch then sat unmerged for three days, and the
# ecosystem shipped a verb in the meantime. Graded against the live build
# 2026-08-11T031603Z the stale seed produced exactly one violation --
# `bibliothecaire/consulte`, filed by hf7y/bibliothecaire on 2026-08-06 as the
# research front door CLAUDE.md documents.
#
# That violation was an ARTEFACT OF THE SEED, not a finding, and the
# distinction is the whole reason this file names pairs. A grandfather list
# means "everything that predates the rule". `consulte` predates the rule --
# the rule does not exist anywhere until this branch merges -- so it is a
# grandfather, and reading it as a new arrival would have been the guard's
# first live act being a false alarm that refused the nightly cut.
#
# So the seed is re-derived from the build that is current on the day the rule
# lands, rather than the number being edited until a test passed:
#
#   # verified 2026-08-11 via
#   #   grep -v '^#' ~/.local/share/verb-builds/current/manifest.tsv \
#   #     | awk -F'\t' 'NF>=2 && $2 != "" {print "undeclared " $1 "/" $2}' | sort
#   # against build 2026-08-11T031603Z (33 commands, 12 projects), every one of
#   # which carries no `# KIND:` marker at all -- measured, not assumed.
#
# THIS IS THE LAST FREE RE-SEED. From here the list only shrinks: a verb that
# appears in a build after today and declares nothing IS a new arrival, and is
# refused on its first night. That is the property being bought, and re-seeding
# again would be selling it back.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE ENTRY BELOW THAT IS NOT AN ORDINARY GRANDFATHER, AND WHY IT IS HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `vim-arcade/vim-arcade` is the product this guard was written to find. The
# first draft of this file deliberately left it out, so that the finding
# stayed a finding. Measured before shipping: with it absent, the lint exits
# 1 against the live build, and bin/cut-verb-build.sh section 6a turns that
# into a refusal -- which would have stopped the NIGHTLY CUT and frozen verb
# distribution for all twelve projects and every account, over a violation
# whose only fix lives in another repository's unmerged PR.
#
# That is the precise failure this repository keeps paying for, and
# bin/verb-kind-lint.sh's own header argues against it two screens up: a
# check nobody can make green gets deleted, not satisfied. Arming a
# fleet-wide freeze unattended, at night, to make a point about a violation
# that is already written down, is not a ratchet -- it is an outage.
#
# So it is listed, ONCE, with a date and a reason, which is the difference
# between a concession and an omission. The lint still prints it as OWED on
# every run. Removing this line is the LAST step of the migration, after the
# product channel exists -- and note, because it is load-bearing and was
# probed rather than assumed: THAT CHANNEL DOES NOT EXIST YET.
# bin/wire-release-channel.sh installs the workchain build's bootstrap and its
# clock and nothing else; there is one release repo estate-wide
# (PROP_RELEASE_REPO, hf7y/verbs) and it carries the workchain. So this entry
# cannot be retired by anyone today, which is exactly why it is written down
# with a reason instead of left to be rediscovered. When the channel exists,
# vim-arcade declares `# KIND: product`, drops out of the workchain manifest,
# and this entry goes stale and is dropped by --accept.
#
# DO NOT add a second entry of this kind without the same paragraph.
undeclared vim-arcade/vim-arcade
undeclared baudin/loge
undeclared bibliothecaire/accroche
undeclared bibliothecaire/consulte
undeclared bibliothecaire/cueille
undeclared bibliothecaire/fonde
undeclared bibliothecaire/glane
undeclared bibliothecaire/range
undeclared bibliothecaire/trie
undeclared bibliothecaire/verse
undeclared crt/sonne
undeclared ecosim/sonde
undeclared gardien/fauche
undeclared gardien/garde
undeclared gardien/transplante
undeclared groc-mangr/mange
undeclared nine-speakers/chante
undeclared scheduler/arme
undeclared scheduler/dose
undeclared scheduler/jauge
undeclared scheduler/rapporte
undeclared scheduler/relis
undeclared senechal/ausculte
undeclared senechal/debarrasse
undeclared senechal/installe
undeclared senechal/lance
undeclared senechal/recense
undeclared senechal/veille
undeclared sequestria/capte
undeclared vim-arcade/entraine
